    (iii) WHAT THE SOURCES CAN DELIVER (a fault bound): each LM5176 5.1 V stage's average loop limits between 7.0957 and 9.5960 A (VSNS
         43 to 57 mV PRINTED, SNVSAI1D, over the 6 mOhm shunt at 1 %, DECLARED; record l9t5 prints 9.5960 A); with this record's
         slotlm draft all four leads are such stages: 4 x 9.596 = 38.38 A (with Layer 9's U601 at its output bound 5.15 A: 43.53 A).
         One stage in its limit with the others at the largest state: 25.112 A.
    WHICH ONE THE DECLARATION HOLDS, AND WHY: the peak holds (i), named an upper bound in its note; the typical holds the sum of the
    leads' typicals; the loads stay the union of the leads' allocations, with the PoE return added.
      - (i) is the only figure that is true by construction: the return cannot carry more than its leads are declared to carry,
        and those declarations are Layer 5's contract at both ends of each lead. Derived in the generator, it follows a lead
        that is declared again; typed, it went stale on 28 September (S-98) and again with fans12.
      - (ii) against (i), like for like (the PoE stage is off in the budget, so the 5 V leads alone): with Layer 9's draft composed
        the largest state with every start at once is 26.166 A against 27.3108 A: inside. Without that draft it is 26.127 A against
        25.80 A: OVER by 0.327 A, because the device lead declares 6.0 A where the budget has 7.316 A in that state (7.4717 A on
        C-DEV rev 1). That is I-03 itself (OPEN, Layer 9's): the return follows what that lead declares and does not paper over it.
      - (iii) is a fault current of four independent limits at once; intent.py's own rule puts a fault current in the note,
        not in the peak (the barrel rule PI-003 reads the peak). One stage in its limit is inside (i).
      - what the peak is NOT: a claim that 27.91 A flows. The copper rules solve the return's mesh at the declared loads,
        22.113 A with the PoE return; the peak is what a barrel is judged at.

3. THE CAPACITY BASIS AS DRAWN
3a. THE GROUND CONDUCTORS BETWEEN BOARDS A AND B, read on both committed netlists (v2/ecad/pcb-a-power-a23/out/pcb-a-power.net, v2/ecad/pcb-b-compute-b19/out/pcb-b-compute.net):
      lead contacts, pin 2 on GND: board A J_54V, J_5V_DEV, J_5V_S1, J_5V_S2, J_5V_S3; board B J_54V, J_5V_DEV, J_5V_S1, J_5V_S2, J_5V_S3: the same five
      ribbon conductors on GND: J_AB1 pins 3,4,5,6,7,8,22,23,24 (board A) and 3,4,5,6,7,8,22,23,24 (board B); J_AB2 pins 3,4,5,6,7,8,9,10 and 3,4,5,6,7,8,9,10: 17 conductors, the same on both boards
      so the two boards' grounds are ONE net joined by five lead contacts and seventeen ribbon conductors in parallel (six leads
      with Layer 9's draft). Nothing makes a lead's pin 2 carry its own rail's current: the return divides by resistance.
      NOT IN THE MODEL (each takes current off the conductors below; none has a held resistance): the shields of the GNSS and
      LoRa pigtails if board A bonds its jacks to GND, and each board's own plane resistance, taken as one node a board.
3b. THE MAKERS' PRINTED FIGURES
      JST VH (v2/vendor/connectors/jst-vh-catalogue.pdf, catalogue, revision not printed): 10 A 'when using AWG #16 with the standard type header' (PRINTED); 7 A
        'when using AWG #18 with the shrouded type header' (PRINTED; the fitted B2P-VH is the standard header, so J_54V's AWG 18 lead
        has NO STATED RATING, Layer 5's own INCONCLUSIVE); range to +105 C including the rise (PRINTED); contact resistance 10 mOhm
        maximum initial, 20 mOhm after test (PRINTED; NO MINIMUM and no typical); conductors 1.25 mm2 (AWG 16) and 0.83 mm2 (AWG 18)
        (PRINTED). Its note (PRINTED): 'Do not branch in parallel current which exceeds the rated current. If branched in parallel,
        current imbalance or other problems may occur. If it is absolutely necessary to branch such a large current in parallel,
        design the circuits without causing any imbalance and provide extra margin for each circuit.'
      Wurth WR-CAB ribbon (v2/vendor/connectors/wurth-wr-cab-ribbon-63912615521cab.pdf): rated current 1 A a conductor at 25 C ambient, 'the current rating may decrease due to the derating
        effect at higher temperatures' with no curve, operating to +105 C, 'the operating temperature is comprised of ambient
        temperature and temperature rise of the component' (PRINTED); conductor resistance 237 Ohm/km maximum, AWG 28 (PRINTED).
      Wurth WR-BHD IDC socket (v2/vendor/connectors/wurth-wr-bhd-idc-socket-61202623021.pdf): 1 A a contact, contact resistance 20 mOhm maximum, NO MINIMUM (PRINTED). Box header: 3 A at 25 C.
      Amass XT60 (the pack connector's make, J_BATT on board E; XT60-F and XT60-M). The held specification V1.2 (v2/vendor/battery/amass-xt60-spec-tme.pdf):
        rated current 30 A, momentary 60 A, contact resistance 0.55 mOhm (no limit sign), 12 AWG, 1000 uses, -20 to 120 C (PRINTED).
        The specification 2021V1 the distributor serves for the same part (v2/vendor/battery/amass-xt60-spec-2021v1-lcsc-c98733.pdf, filed this round):
        35 A MAX with 12 AWG at a temperature rise under 85 C, contact resistance at most 1.0 mOhm, service life 100 times, the same
        range (PRINTED). Neither prints a contact resistance after test, a minimum or a derating curve. The rows below take the
        LOWER rated current (30 A), the HIGHER contact resistance (1.0 mOhm, a printed limit, taken as the contact's maximum over
        its printed life: INFERRED, the sheet does not say so) and the SHORTER life.
      THE COPPER'S TEMPERATURE, both ends: 76.25 C, the inside air in E5's dwell (L4-E12, MODELED; 71.25 C in E3-O), and -20 C, the
        envelope's cold end in use (v2/ecad/tools/pcb_envelope.yaml ambient_c.in_use.min: the harness at ambient at a cold start). A conductor's largest
        share falls as the copper warms (its own resistance is copper, the others' is mostly contact) and the ground shift rises:
        each maximum is at one end, so both ends are enumerated. The budget's largest states at either end are conservative pairings.
      THE RATINGS AT THE INSIDE AIR. No maker here prints a derating curve. Beside each PRINTED rating the rows carry the LEAST
        rating consistent with its sheet at that air (INFERRED): the part may add (range top - air) K, and heating goes as the
        current squared (ASSUMPTION). The ribbon: its 1 A is printed at 25 C and its range ends at +105 C, so its rise at 1 A is
        at most 80 K and at 76.25 C it carries at least 0.5995 A. The XT60: 35 A at a rise under 85 K and a range to 120 C give
        25.11 A at 76.25 C. The VH: no ambient is printed for its 10 A; taken at 25 C (ASSUMPTION) the same rule gives 5.995 A (with a
        30 K rise at the rating, round 7's ASSUMPTION, 9.789 A). At the cold end every printed rating applies as printed.
      THE GROUND SHIFT LAYER 9'S LDOS ALLOW: record l9t5 (round 3, the copy) prints the LDOs' input 4.6580 V against the AP2112K's need
        3.749 V with 0.0512 V of shift taken, and holds 'while the ground shift between the boards stays under 0.960 V'; the recheck V3
        adds the regulator's line and load regulation and prints about 0.9418 V. The rows below are judged at the smaller, 0.9418 V.
